# The other flip-flop candidate: a pulse latch. The data repeater M is locked
# by K except for a few ticks after each rising clock edge, when K's input L
# goes low:
#   L = NOT(clock delayed 2) OR (clock delayed 8)
# The clock enters at N, passes repeater A into stone S1 (strongly powered, so
# the torch T on it goes out two ticks after the edge) and on through the
# setting-3 repeater R into stone S2, which powers the L dust beside it eight
# ticks after the edge. Between those two moments L is low, K unlocks, and M
# samples D. The stones over the data row stop the L dust at y=2 from touching
# it diagonally. Measured in stage 8 (see PLAN.md): it works, but samples the
# data late (up to 7 ticks after the edge), so its hold time is 11 against the
# master/slave's 5, and it needs a clock period of 16 against 12. Kept for the
# record; `dff` is the one the builds use.
tile dff_alt
size 10 4 5
port W in 0 1 2
port N in 2 1 0
port E out 9 1 2
function dff
delay 8
edge rising
timing setup -5 hold 11 clk_to_q 8 min_high 4 min_low 2 min_period 16

legend
  G minecraft:glass
  # minecraft:stone
  - minecraft:redstone_wire
  A minecraft:repeater[facing=west,delay=1]
  R minecraft:repeater[facing=west,delay=3]
  K minecraft:repeater[facing=north,delay=1]
  M minecraft:repeater[facing=west,delay=1]
  T minecraft:redstone_torch

layer 0
  ..G.......
  ..GGGGGG..
  GG..GGGGGG
  .GGGGGGGG.
  ..........
layer 1
  ..-.......
  ..-A#R#-..
  --..###K--
  .------M-.
  ..........
layer 2
  ..........
  ....T.-...
  ....---...
  ....###...
  ..........
layer 3
  ..........
  ..........
  ..........
  ..........
  ..........
